window.docContents[160].resolve({"/1___-Introduction/Chapter-1___-Introduction/#Chapter1___Introduction___heading-1":{"contents":"Very roughly speaking, representation theory studies symmetry in linear spaces. It is a beautiful mathematical subject which has many applications, ranging from number theory and combinatorics to geometry, probability theory, quantum mechanics, and quantum field theory.\n\nRepresentation theory was born in 1896 in the work of the German mathematician F. G. Frobenius. This work was triggered by a letter to Frobenius by R. Dedekind. In this letter Dedekind made the following observation: take the multiplication table of a finite group G and turn it into a matrix X_G by replacing every entry g of this table by a variable x_g. Then the determinant of X_G factors into a product of irreducible polynomials in \\{x_g\\}, each of which occurs with multiplicity equal to its degree. Dedekind checked this surprising fact in a few special cases but could not prove it in general. So he gave this problem to Frobenius. In order to find a solution of this problem (which we will explain below), Frobenius created the representation theory of finite groups.\n\nThe goal of this book is to give a “holistic” introduction to representation theory, presenting it as a unified subject which studies representations of associative algebras and treating the representation theories of groups, Lie algebras, and quivers as special cases. It is designed as a textbook for advanced undergraduate and beginning graduate students and should be accessible to students with a strong background in linear algebra and a basic knowledge of abstract algebra. Theoretical material in this book is supplemented by many problems and exercises which touch upon a lot of additional topics; the more difficult exercises are provided with hints.\n\nThe book covers a number of standard topics in representation theory of groups, associative algebras, Lie algebras, and quivers. For a more detailed treatment of these topics, we refer the reader to the textbooks [S], [FH], and [CR]. We mostly follow [FH], with the exception of the sections discussing quivers, which follow [BGP], and the sections on homological algebra and finite dimensional algebras, for which we recommend [W] and [CR], respectively.\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00091. Introduction\u0009Chapter 1: Introduction\u0009Chapter 1: Introduction","header":"Chapter 1: Introduction","id":"/1___-Introduction/Chapter-1___-Introduction/#Chapter1___Introduction___heading-1"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___14___-Tensor-products-and-duals-of-representations-of-Lie-algebras/Dual-representation-of-a-Lie-algebra/#Chapter2___Definition2___14___2___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.14. Tensor products and duals of representations of Lie algebras\u0009Dual representation of a Lie algebra\u0009Dual representation of a Lie algebra","header":"Formalization","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___14___-Tensor-products-and-duals-of-representations-of-Lie-algebras/Dual-representation-of-a-Lie-algebra/#Chapter2___Definition2___14___2___formalization"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Representation-of-an-algebra-_LPAR_left-A-module_RPAR_/#Chapter2___Definition2___3___1___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.3. Representations\u0009Representation of an algebra (left A-module)\u0009Representation of an algebra (left A-module)","header":"Formalization","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___3___-Representations/Representation-of-an-algebra-_LPAR_left-A-module_RPAR_/#Chapter2___Definition2___3___1___formalization"},"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___7___-Examples-of-algebras/Center___-ideals___-and-representations-of-the-q-Weyl-algebra/#Chapter2___Problem2___7___5":{"contents":"Problem 2.7.5. Let q be a nonzero complex number, and let A be the q-Weyl algebra over \\mathbb{C}.\n\n(a) What is the center of A for different q? If q is not a root of unity, what are the two-sided ideals in A?\n\n(b) For which q does this algebra have finite dimensional representations?\n\nHint: Use determinants.\n\n(c) Find all finite dimensional irreducible representations of A for such q.\n\nHint: This is similar to part (c) of the previous problem.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00092. Basic notions of representation theory\u00092.7. Examples of algebras\u0009Center, ideals, and representations of the q-Weyl algebra","header":"Center, ideals, and representations of the q-Weyl algebra","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___7___-Examples-of-algebras/Center___-ideals___-and-representations-of-the-q-Weyl-algebra/#Chapter2___Problem2___7___5"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras/Dual-representation/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras--Dual-representation--Dual-representation--Formalization--Primary-declarations":{"contents":"The module structure of the linear dual over the opposite ring induced by a compatible action\non the original module.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Definition3.3.2; role=primary]\n\nThe action of an element of the opposite ring on a linear dual evaluates by applying the\nfunctional after the corresponding action on the vector.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter3/Definition3.3.2; role=primary]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00093. General results of representation theory\u00093.3. Representations of direct sums of matrix algebras\u0009Dual representation\u0009Dual representation\u0009Formalization","header":"Primary declarations","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras/Dual-representation/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--3___-General-results-of-representation-theory--3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras--Dual-representation--Dual-representation--Formalization--Primary-declarations"},"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___8___-Character-tables___-examples/Section-4___8___-Character-tables-___-definition-and-examples-of-S_3___-A_4/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___8___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00094. Representations of finite groups: Basic results\u00094.8. Character tables, examples\u0009Section 4.8: Character tables — definition and examples of S_3, A_4\u0009Section 4.8: Character tables — definition and examples of S_3, A_4","header":"Formalization","id":"/4___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Basic-results/4___8___-Character-tables___-examples/Section-4___8___-Character-tables-___-definition-and-examples-of-S_3___-A_4/#Chapter4___Introduction_4___8___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___/Definition-of-lexicographic-ordering-on-partitions/#Chapter5___Discussion_lexicographic_ordering___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.13. Proof of the classification theorem for representations of $S_n$\u0009Definition of lexicographic ordering on partitions\u0009Definition of lexicographic ordering on partitions","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___13___-Proof-of-the-classification-theorem-for-representations-of-___S_n___/Definition-of-lexicographic-ordering-on-partitions/#Chapter5___Discussion_lexicographic_ordering___formalization"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___21___-Schur-polynomials/Character-expansion-in-terms-of-Schur-polynomials/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___21___-Schur-polynomials--Character-expansion-in-terms-of-Schur-polynomials--Character-expansion-in-terms-of-Schur-polynomials--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Expresses a partition power-sum polynomial as a finite sum of partition polynomials weighted by expansion coefficients.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter5/Proposition5.21.1; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.21. Schur polynomials\u0009Character expansion in terms of Schur polynomials\u0009Character expansion in terms of Schur polynomials\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___21___-Schur-polynomials/Character-expansion-in-terms-of-Schur-polynomials/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results--5___21___-Schur-polynomials--Character-expansion-in-terms-of-Schur-polynomials--Character-expansion-in-terms-of-Schur-polynomials--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Historical-note-on-Burnside___s-theorem-and-proof-plan/#Chapter5___Discussion_after_Theorem5___4___3":{"contents":"This famous result in group theory was proved by the British mathematician William Burnside in the early 20th century, using representation theory (see Section 5.5 and [Cu]). Here is this proof, presented in modern language.\n\nBefore proving Burnside's theorem, we will prove several other results which are of independent interest.\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.4. Burnside's theorem\u0009Historical note on Burnside's theorem and proof plan","header":"Historical note on Burnside's theorem and proof plan","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Historical-note-on-Burnside___s-theorem-and-proof-plan/#Chapter5___Discussion_after_Theorem5___4___3"},"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Section-5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___4___formalization":{"contents":"\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00095. Representations of finite groups: Further results\u00095.4. Burnside's theorem\u0009Section 5.4: Burnside's theorem\u0009Section 5.4: Burnside's theorem","header":"Formalization","id":"/5___-Representations-of-finite-groups___-Further-results/5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/Section-5___4___-Burnside___s-theorem/#Chapter5___Introduction_5___4___formalization"},"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___5___-Gabriel___s-theorem/Theorem-6___5___2___-Gabriel___s-theorem/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___5___-Gabriel___s-theorem--Theorem-6___5___2___-Gabriel___s-theorem--Theorem-6___5___2___-Gabriel___s-theorem--Formalization--Supporting-declarations":{"contents":"Under the stated matrix hypothesis, only finitely many integer vectors satisfy the specified predicate.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Theorem6.5.2; role=supporting]\n\nA nonnegative nonzero integer vector satisfies the specified predicate when its dot product with the indicated matrix product is two.* Etingof et al., Introduction to Representation Theory [book-ref=Chapter6/Theorem6.5.2; role=supporting]\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\u00096. Quiver representations\u00096.5. Gabriel's theorem\u0009Theorem 6.5.2: Gabriel's theorem\u0009Theorem 6.5.2: Gabriel's theorem\u0009Formalization","header":"Supporting declarations","id":"/6___-Quiver-representations/6___5___-Gabriel___s-theorem/Theorem-6___5___2___-Gabriel___s-theorem/#Introduction-to-Representation-Theory--6___-Quiver-representations--6___5___-Gabriel___s-theorem--Theorem-6___5___2___-Gabriel___s-theorem--Theorem-6___5___2___-Gabriel___s-theorem--Formalization--Supporting-declarations"}});